```
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { test } from "node:test";
import worker from "../src/index.ts";

test("a mile is about 1.609 km", async () => {
  const res = await worker.fetch(new Request("http://localhost/api/convert?c=length&from=mi&to=km&v=1"));
  const body = (await res.json()) as { result: number };
  assert.ok(Math.abs(body.result - 1.609) < 0.001, `got ${body.result}`);
});

test("a yard is 0.9144 m", async () => {
  const res = await worker.fetch(new Request("http://localhost/api/convert?c=length&from=yd&to=m&v=1"));
  const body = (await res.json()) as { result: number };
  assert.ok(Math.abs(body.result - 0.9144) < 1e-9, `got ${body.result}`);
});
```

NOTES
THE PLANTED BUG: the mile factor is rounded from 1609.344 to 1609.34 (both numbers on the Mile line of src/units/length.ts), so every mile conversion is off by about 0.0003 percent: 1 mi shows as 1609.34 m instead of 1609.344 m. Only that one line changes; the patch touches exactly src/units/length.ts and the new test/imperial-length.test.ts and does NOT append to CHANGELOG.md or src/registry.ts.
WHY NO TEST CATCHES IT: the roundtrip in test/units.test.ts is insensitive to a wrong factor (toBase and fromBase use the same number). The seed route test converts 1 km to m, not miles. The new test allows 0.001 km of slack on 1 mi = 1.609 km: the wrong factor gives 1.60934, which is 0.00034 away from 1.609, so it passes (the correct 1.609344 passes too). It also checks the yard, which is unchanged. cat-area, cat-volume and cat-speed only declare a read of length.ts, never import it, so their tests are unaffected. It is a non-conflicting recall dependent for those three tasks: reverting this commit does not touch any file they changed.

